1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,543
Part two — the organization.

2
00:00:03,603 --> 00:00:09,147
Same engine, different subject:
people, process, and what they cost.

3
00:00:09,207 --> 00:00:13,550
Chapter fifteen — an
organization is a model too.

4
00:00:13,610 --> 00:00:20,656
A workspace can hold an organization
instead of an architecture.

5
00:00:20,716 --> 00:00:27,661
The lenses change with it:
an org chart, and swimlanes.

6
00:00:27,721 --> 00:00:29,683
And so does the palette.

7
00:00:29,743 --> 00:00:40,188
People and roles, meetings, documents, automation
— the things an organization is made of.

8
00:00:40,248 --> 00:00:48,564
Every role is modelled, not drawn.

9
00:00:48,624 --> 00:00:56,169
Headcount, allocation, hours
a week, and a loaded rate.

10
00:00:56,229 --> 00:01:05,175
Which gives you capacity in hours a
month, and cost in dollars a month.

11
00:01:05,235 --> 00:01:15,180
The reporting line is a real edge, so span
of control and bus factor fall out of it.

12
00:01:15,240 --> 00:01:23,184
At the bottom, the same always-live
strip — for an organization.

13
00:01:23,244 --> 00:01:34,188
Fifty-six people. One point six million a month.
Nine managers, average span three point six.

14
00:01:34,248 --> 00:01:45,038
And Design builds one from a description,
the way Architect does for systems.

15
00:01:45,098 --> 00:01:48,902
An org chart that costs itself.
